                            %mo =
                            %mn 1
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h' . \eta_A = M \alpha . h_A . M\eta_A . \eta_A = M \alpha . h_A . \eta_{MA} . \eta_A = M \alpha . (\eta_{MA} . e_A)^{-1} . \eta_A = M \alpha . 1 . \eta_A = 1
                      \.
              %li
                %p
                  (
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 4
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mn 3
                        %mo ′
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4 \Rightarrow 3'
                  ) This is immediate, since
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi MX
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi μ
                          %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (MX, \mu_X)
                  is always a strict Eilenberg-Moore algebra.
          %p
            Note that when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            are locally
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+category"} discrete
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+adjunctions"} idempotent adjunctions
          %p
            In contrast to that situation, however, the lax-idempotent situation is of interest even when the adjunction is monadic or comonadic. In the monadic case, the implication
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 4
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mn 4
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4 \Rightarrow 4'
            means that the induced 2-comonad on the 2-category of algebras for a lax-idempotent 2-monad is colax-idempotent. Its (pseudo) coalgebras are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+algebras"} continuous algebras
            for the original 2-monad.
          %h2#references References
          %p As “KZ-adjunctions”:
          %ul
            %li
              J. Meseguer, U. Montanari, and V. Sassone.
              = succeed "." do
                %em ω-Inductive completion of monoidal categories and infinite petri net computations
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://eprints.soton.ac.uk/261872/1/w-ind-compl.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            As “KZ-adjointness” (defined by both (1)
            %em and
            (6)):
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marta+Bunge"} Marta Bunge
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathon+Funk"} Jonathon Funk
              = succeed "." do
                %em Singular Coverings of Toposes
          %p As “lax idempotent 2-adjunctions”:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maria+Manuel+Clementino"} Maria Manuel Clementino
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fernando+Lucatelli+Nunes"} Fernando Lucatelli Nunes
              = succeed "." do
                %em Lax comma 2-categories and admissible 2-functors
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.03132"} arXiv:2002.03132
              (2020).
          %p
            However, note in this paper that the authors use “lax idempotent 2-comonad” for what, according to the convention for lax morphisms for coalgebras on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+morphism"} lax morphism
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                colax idempotent 2-comonad
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/colax+idempotent+2-comonad"} ?
